# AI Coding Assistant Guidelines

This file defines strict expectations for AI coding assistants (Claude, Grok, etc.) when working on this project and other coding projects. These rules exist to maintain high code quality, avoid technical debt, and ensure reliable, maintainable results across diverse technology stacks and long-running personal or collaborative endeavors.

**How I Work With AI (Important Context for Assistants):**

- User primarily operates in a **supervisory / director role**. User expects the AI to handle detailed implementation, research, and problem-solving while User provides high-level direction, reviews outputs, requests changes, and makes final architectural or design decisions.
- The strict rules in this file exist so the AI can produce consistent, high-quality work with less back-and-forth on fundamentals, allowing the human supervisor to focus creative energy and oversight where it matters most.
- When User requests changes, corrections, or refinements, treat them as high priority. Iterate promptly and thoroughly until the deliverable meets the complete, production-ready standard.
- User values complete, production-ready code and artifacts over rapid but incomplete or placeholder progress. The goal is always a drop-in, fully functional contribution that advances the project without leaving loose ends.

---

## 1. Core Philosophy: Complete, Production-Ready Code Only

- **No deferrals, no stubs, no placeholders, no TODOs, no "implement later", etc.**

  - Deliver **full, complete, wired-up, working code** (or equivalent artifacts such as configurations, schemas, scripts, or documentation) every time.
  - If a feature, class, method, module, endpoint, data model, or subsystem is requested, implement it fully — including all necessary supporting code, registrations/initializations, dependency wiring, error handling, logging where appropriate, tests, and integrations with existing systems.
  - Partial implementations that "work for the happy path" but leave error cases, edge conditions, or integration points unfinished are not acceptable.

- **Dependency-first implementation**

  - If the code you are writing depends on classes, methods, services, schemas, configurations, or systems that do not yet exist, **build and deliver those dependencies first** (or as part of the same complete delivery) before or alongside the original request.
  - Never leave broken references, missing imports, unresolved symbols, or "we'll add this later" gaps in the delivered code. The project must build and run cleanly after each delivery.

- **One-shot completeness**

  - Prefer solutions that can be dropped in and work immediately (or with minimal configuration) over incremental "we'll build it up over multiple steps" approaches, unless the user has explicitly requested an incremental plan with clear milestones.
  - When a large feature naturally decomposes into logical, independently valuable slices, deliver each slice as a complete, tested, documented unit rather than a skeleton.

**Rationale and Implications**: This philosophy minimizes context-switching and technical debt in long-running projects. It ensures every AI contribution is immediately usable for testing, integration, or further development, building trust and momentum. For solo developers balancing multiple ambitious initiatives, it reduces the risk of "almost done" features lingering and becoming sources of bugs or forgotten assumptions later. Edge case: When requirements are genuinely exploratory, confirm with the user whether a prototype or spike is acceptable, but still deliver it in a clean, self-contained form that can be evolved or discarded without polluting the main codebase.

